# Module 5 Completed: Subtitle Overlay

The subtitle overlay functionality has been successfully implemented! Here is a breakdown of the architecture and how to test it.

## 1. Files Changed & Added
- **`manifest.json`**: Added `content_scripts` to automatically inject `overlay.js` and `overlay.css` into all webpages.
- **`offscreen/offscreen.js`**: Added a `websocketClient.onmessage` listener. When it receives a JSON transcript from the Python backend, it forwards it to the service worker as a `TRANSCRIPT_EVENT`.
- **`background/service-worker.js`**: Added a message router that catches the `TRANSCRIPT_EVENT` and forwards it directly to the active tab that is currently being captured.
- **`content/overlay.js` [NEW]**: The core UI logic. It continually scans for the active HTML5 `<video>` element, anchors a floating subtitle container to it, and updates the text dynamically when transcripts arrive.
- **`content/overlay.css` [NEW]**: High-contrast, responsive styling (white text, dark translucent background, rounded corners) that ensures subtitles are readable without obscuring video content.

## 2. Overlay Architecture

### Video Detection
The content script uses a `setInterval` loop to scan the DOM for the active `<video>` element (preferring videos that are currently playing). If you navigate to a new YouTube video, the script will automatically detect the new video element and re-attach the overlay.

### Responsive Positioning
Instead of hardcoding screen coordinates, the overlay uses a `requestAnimationFrame` loop to constantly measure the `getBoundingClientRect()` of the active video. 
- In normal mode, it uses `position: fixed` to perfectly float over the exact bounding box of the video, regardless of scrolling or window resizing.
- In fullscreen mode, it detects `document.fullscreenElement`, changes its positioning to `position: absolute`, and moves itself inside the fullscreen container to ensure it stays on top.

### Transcript Rendering
- **Partial Transcripts**: As you speak, partial transcripts immediately replace the current text, creating a smooth, real-time typing effect.
- **Final Transcripts**: When a final transcript arrives, it replaces the text and starts a 4-second cleanup timer. 
- **Stale Removal**: If no new speech arrives within 4 seconds, the subtitle smoothly fades out, ensuring stale text doesn't sit on the screen permanently.

## Testing Instructions

> [!IMPORTANT]
> Because `manifest.json` was modified to include a new content script, **you MUST reload the extension** in Chrome before testing!
> 1. Go to `chrome://extensions/`
> 2. Find the "Live Subtitle" extension
> 3. Click the "Reload" icon (the circular arrow)

### Test the Pipeline
1. Start your local Python backend (`python pipeline_server.py`).
2. Open a YouTube video and start the Chrome extension capture.
3. You should immediately see the subtitles appearing at the bottom of the video as the audio plays.
4. Try resizing the window, scrolling down, and entering/exiting Fullscreen mode. The subtitles should stick perfectly to the video!
5. When the speaker pauses, the subtitle should fade away after 4 seconds.

*Note: Since this uses generic HTML5 `<video>` detection, it will work on YouTube and should also work out-of-the-box on Netflix and most other video players!*
